<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Scandinavia and the Baltic</p><h1 class="st-title">Hadeland</h1><p class="st-meta">750 CE – 1020 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Small inland kingdom on Lake Randsfjorden, linked in saga tradition to Halfdan the Black and ended when Olaf Haraldsson removed the Uppland kings.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Hadeland, a farming district around the southern end of Lake <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/randsfjorden">Randsfjorden</a>, was one of the petty kingdoms of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upplands-norway">Upplands</a> of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/snorri-sturluson">Snorri Sturluson's</a> sagas say it was won by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/halfdan-the-black">Halfdan the Black</a>, father of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harald-fairhair">Harald Fairhair</a>, in the 9th century.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Little is known of its government. The sagas tell that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/halfdan-the-black">Halfdan the Black</a> drowned when his sleigh broke through the ice on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/randsfjorden">Randsfjorden</a>, and that the districts quarrelled over his body, which was divided and buried in several places. Local kings continued to hold the district under the Norwegian high kings.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Around 1018 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olaf-ii-of-norway">Olaf Haraldsson</a> defeated a coalition of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upplands-norway">Uppland</a> kings, including the king of Hadeland, and took their lands into his kingdom. Hadeland became part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-norway">Kingdom of Norway</a>, and the twin medieval churches at Granavollen are among its later landmarks.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/hadeland.jpg" alt="Hadeland" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: User: Mahlum · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Randsfjorden_fra_Jevnaker.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>750 CE – 1020 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Norse paganism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Norse</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/norway">Norway</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of Hadeland</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=885&amp;focus=hadeland" aria-label="Hadeland on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4881 126 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Hadeland lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5225 425h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5201 382h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5201 382h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Norway. <a href="/atlas?year=885&amp;focus=hadeland">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
